devfreq: report demand rather than duty cycle, and scale by default

A utilization governor asks what fraction of the window the device spent
executing, and treats a device idle half the time as one that can be slowed
down for free. That holds when the device is the bottleneck. This one is not:
an inference is about 2.3 ms of NPU time inside a 9 ms pipeline, so even at
full load and the highest rate the busiest core measures ~50% - and at the
lowest rate only ~64%, never reaching the 85% simple_ondemand needs to hold.
It therefore walks to the lowest OPP for every workload measured, which costs
29% throughput on mobilenet and 27% on a chained model, because the NPU is
still on the critical path even while it is idle half the time.

There is no backlog to measure instead. Pending tasks only exist while a job
is on the hardware, so time-with-work-queued tracks time-executing to within
a fraction of a percent and carries no extra information.

So report demand. Any execution inside a sampling window means the device was
wanted during that window and a higher rate would finish the work sooner, so
the window is reported as fully busy. The rate is only allowed to fall after
several consecutive windows with no work at all, which stops the gap between
inferences from causing a transition. dvfs_demand_metric=N restores the duty
cycle metric for comparison.

With a metric that can reach the top of the range, load-based scaling is worth
having, so the device now comes up on simple_ondemand rather than userspace.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

